Your rules for this feature, as the builders will follow them (each one applies to every case like it, not just the example):
  1. Whenever a refund is approved, for any reason, the refund amount is the sum of item prices times quantities minus the discount code value. The coupon value itself is not returned. For example, a ₹2,000 order with a ₹300 coupon refunds ₹1,700, and a ₹1,500 bag refunds ₹1,500.
  2. Whenever a refund is approved, the money goes back the way the order was paid. Card orders are refunded to the card, and store-credit orders are refunded as store credit.
  3. Whenever a customer requests a change-of-mind refund, the refund is the full refund amount if the request is made within 14 days after delivery (day 14 included). It is half the refund amount from day 15 through day 30 after delivery, and nothing after day 30. Shipping is never returned. For example, a ₹2,000 saree requested 10 days after delivery gets a full refund.
  4. Whenever a customer requests a refund because the goods arrived damaged, the refund is the full refund amount plus the shipping fee if the request is made up to 90 days after delivery. After 90 days there is no refund. For example, ₹3,000 of items plus ₹150 shipping refunds ₹3,150 when requested 20 days after delivery. The 14-day and 30-day tiers do not apply to damaged goods.
  5. Whenever a customer returns items from an order, they may return a single item from the order, such as 1 of 3 shirts. An item that has already been refunded is never refunded a second time.
  6. Whenever an order is placed, it earns 1 loyalty point per full ₹100 of the item total after the coupon is subtracted. Leftover rupees earn nothing, and shipping and tax earn nothing. The order earns points only if that item total after the coupon is at least ₹500 and the order was not paid by store credit. Final-sale orders earn double points. For example, ₹1,250 of items earns 12 points, and ₹2,000 of items with a ₹300 coupon, ₹100 shipping and ₹180 tax earns 17 points (34 if final sale).
